(Lower bound: a chain of primes.) For $0\le i\le d$ let $P_i=(X_1,\ldots,X_i)\subseteq R_0$ ($P_0=0$). By [lem-weil-pbc-power-series-quotient-variables], $R_0/P_i\cong\Lambda[[X_{i+1},\ldots,X_d]]$ via $\pi_i$, which is a nonzero integral domain since $\Lambda$ is one (step 1, [lem-weil-pbc-power-series-domain]); so $P_i$ is a prime ideal. For $i<d$, $P_i\subsetneq P_{i+1}$: $X_{i+1}\in P_{i+1}$, and $X_{i+1}
otin P_i=\ker\pi_i$ because $\pi_i(X_{i+1})=X_{i+1}
e0$. This is a chain $P_0\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq P_d$ of prime ideals of length $d$. If $\Lambda$ is a Cohen ring, moreover $P_d\subsetneq\mathfrak m_0=(p,X_1,\ldots,X_d)$, a prime (maximal) ideal (step 3): $p\in\mathfrak m_0$, and $p
otin P_d=\ker(F\mapsto F(0))$ since $p(0)=p
e0$ in $\Lambda$ (step 1); this extends the chain to length $d+1$. By the definition of Krull dimension [def-weil-pbc-krull-dimension-ring], $\dim R_0\ge n$ ($n$ as in step 6).
